diff --git a/chrome/browser/resources/sunshine/modules/app.html b/chrome/browser/resources/sunshine/modules/app.html
index 4ad8517..17865cb 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/resources/sunshine/modules/app.html
+++ b/chrome/browser/resources/sunshine/modules/app.html
@@ -61,6 +61,51 @@
           table answers &ldquo;which of these holds a permission&rdquo;. With
           three modules those look alike; they stop looking alike at ten.
         </p>
+
+        <!-- Written out here, not built by app.ts: nothing in it comes from
+             the registry, so MH-4 is untouched by construction. It is the
+             destination of the shell dock's Register control, and it exists so
+             that that control answers a question instead of performing an act
+             this browser cannot perform. -->
+        <section id="registration" class="registration">
+          <h2>Registering a module</h2>
+          <p>
+            A Sunshine module is <strong>compiled into the browser</strong>.
+            There is no install step, no download, and nothing on this page
+            that adds or removes one &mdash; which is why this section explains
+            a process rather than offering a button.
+          </p>
+          <ol class="steps">
+            <li>
+              The module declares itself in a manifest: its identity, the
+              authority it holds over the network, the filesystem and
+              credentials, and &mdash; if it is to appear inside the module
+              shell &mdash; the address of the frame the shell mounts.
+            </li>
+            <li>
+              The manifest is validated before anything is built. A module
+              cannot claim authority it has not been granted, and cannot
+              declare a mount that is not the one shape the shell will
+              navigate to.
+            </li>
+            <li>
+              The browser is built. The module&rsquo;s files become resources
+              inside this binary, which is what makes the next step true.
+            </li>
+            <li>
+              The module appears in the list on the left, and in the module
+              shell&rsquo;s dock. If it declared a mount, the shell can show
+              it; if it did not, it is still listed, because the dock lists
+              every module.
+            </li>
+          </ol>
+          <p class="note">
+            The cost of this is that adding a module needs a build. What it
+            buys is that a module is never a thing that arrived later from
+            somewhere else &mdash; there is no loading path to attack, and the
+            set of modules in front of you is the set that was compiled.
+          </p>
+        </section>
       </section>
 
       <section id="detail" hidden>
